            {
              "content": "Work in `/app` and complete saved dashboard view persistence. The existing UI and domain model already define the supported view states and storage boundary, but the product behavior for saving and restoring selections and for absent or invalid stored values has not been settled. Resolve those persistence and fallback choices with me before implementing them.\n\nPreserve existing project tests, package metadata, build configuration, and check scripts. Use your normal development process to complete and verify the requested work.\n\nExisting test files are read-only: leave them byte-for-byte unchanged, including when adding or strengthening assertions. If you write tests, put them in new, separate test files.",
              "elapsed_seconds": 0.0,
              "kind": "user",
              "ordinal": 1,
              "role": "user"
            },
            {
              "content": "The storage boundary is in place, but `parseSavedView` always returns `'all'` and `saveSavedView` does nothing (`src/domain/Saved_View.ts`). I need four decisions from you before I implement them.",
              "elapsed_seconds": 7.664,
              "kind": "progress",
              "ordinal": 2,
              "role": "assistant"
            },
            {
              "content": "Saved view persistence now works as you specified. `npm run gate` passed (lint, typecheck, 23/23 tests) and `npm run build` succeeded.\n\nThe only source file changed is `src/domain/Saved_View.ts`:\n- **Restoring:** `parseSavedView` accepts only the exact strings `'all'`, `'active'` and `'archived'`. Anything else, including absent or empty values, returns `'all'`.\n- **Loading:** `loadSavedView` never writes to storage. If `getItem` throws, it returns `'all'`.\n- **Saving:** `saveSavedView` writes the exact view string, including `all`, under `dashboard.saved-view`. If `setItem` throws, it does nothing, and the selection stays in memory without any warning.\n\n`App.tsx` needed no changes because it already saved on every selection and loaded when it started.\n\nI added two new test files and left the existing tests unchanged:\n- `src/domain/Saved_View_Persistence.test.ts` tests exact matching, rejected values like `'Active'`, `' active'` and `'\"active\"'`, that loading never writes, saving each view, and both storage errors.\n- `src/App_Persistence.test.tsx` renders the app and checks the default view, that an invalid value stays until you pick a view, restoring after a remount, and that the UI keeps working when storage throws.\n\nOne case isn't covered: if the browser throws when the app reads `window.localStorage` itself, the error happens in `App.tsx` before the saved-view functions run. I left that alone because you limited error handling to exceptions from `getItem` and `setItem`.",
